# Migration SQL bytes are hashed: the engine records a checksum per applied
# migration and refuses to run when a file changes. A checkout that rewrites
# line endings (core.autocrlf=true on Windows) would therefore change every
# checksum and make both `migrate up` and TestEmbeddedMigrationsMatchGoldenChecksums
# fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the migration itself.
#
# Pin these to LF so the bytes are identical on every platform.
internal/platform/migrate/sql/mysql/*.sql text eol=lf
internal/platform/migrate/checksums.golden text eol=lf

# Lua scripts are embedded into the binary too (go:embed in the breaker,
# idemstore, lock and ratelimit packages). No checksum covers them, so CRLF
# cannot break `migrate up`; pinning them is about reproducible builds — a
# Windows-built image would otherwise carry different script bytes than a
# CI-built image from the same commit. This is hardening, not correctness:
# scripts/check-eol.sh deliberately leaves Lua out of its mandatory list.
*.lua text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must survive a Windows checkout. With core.autocrlf=true the
# working tree gets CRLF, which turns the shebang into `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`
# and makes strict shells refuse to exec the file at all; scripts that happen
# to run anyway still carry stray \r into every string comparison. The scripts
# under scripts/ are the documented entry points (stack.sh / smoke.sh /
# deploy.sh), so they are pinned rather than left to the local Git config.
*.sh text eol=lf
